<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Quiets the console output the ported code writes while tests run.

  Upstream OpenNLP prints progress and diagnostics straight to System.out and
  System.err: the trainers report iteration and parameter counts, the model
  writers announce what they are writing, and the sample streams warn about
  lines they skip. The port reproduces those calls faithfully, so a test run
  inherits all of it.

  Two things make that worse than it is under Maven. The VSTest MSBuild task
  surfaces anything a test writes to stderr as a build *warning*, prefixed with
  Microsoft.TestPlatform.targets(48,5) - a location in the SDK, not in this
  repo, which makes the messages read like build problems they are not. And
  several fixtures are read twice by design (LemmatizerEvaluatorTest builds a
  predicted and an expected stream over the same file), so each message lands
  more than once.

  The noisiest single case is opennlp/tools/lemmatizer/output.txt, whose
  sentence separator is a line holding one tab rather than an empty line.
  DummyLemmaSampleStream only stops on "", so all 18 separators fall through to
  its "Skipping corrupt line" branch. Upstream behaves the same way: Java's
  "\t".split("\t") is length 0 and .NET's is length 2, and neither is the 4
  fields the check wants, so this is upstream noise reproduced rather than a
  defect. The fixture is byte-identical to upstream and stays that way.

  Suppressing at this layer rather than in the ported code keeps every
  System.out/System.err call matching its Java counterpart, which is what makes
  the next 1.9.x rebase cheap.

  Not applied automatically: dotnet test only reads this when pointed at it, so
  CI and anyone who wants the full output are unaffected. Pass it with the
  settings switch:

    dotnet test src/NOpenNLP.Tools.Tests -s src/NOpenNLP.Tools.Tests/NOpenNLP.Tools.Tests.runsettings
-->
<RunSettings>

  <NUnit>
    <!--
      Stops the NUnit adapter forwarding what a test writes to Console.Out and
      Console.Error into the runner output. This is the setting that does the
      work: the platform's own RunConfiguration/CaptureTraceOutput was measured
      against this suite and made no difference, because the output comes
      through the adapter rather than through the platform's trace capture, so
      it is deliberately not set here.

      A failing test is unaffected, and was checked rather than assumed: a
      deliberately failing probe still reported its assertion message, its stack
      trace, and its own Standard Output. The suppression only takes effect for
      tests that pass, so nothing is lost when debugging a failure.
    -->
    <ConsoleOut>0</ConsoleOut>
  </NUnit>

</RunSettings>
